A **vector** is a pair of numbers (x, y). Games use it for a **position** (where), a **velocity** (how fast, and which way) and an **acceleration** (how the velocity changes). Unity has `Vector2` and `Vector3` structs; this course uses a small one of its own, and the snippets from here on rely on it:

```csharp
public readonly struct Vector2
{
    public readonly float X, Y;
    public Vector2(float x, float y) { X = x; Y = y; }

    public static Vector2 operator +(Vector2 a, Vector2 b) => new(a.X + b.X, a.Y + b.Y);
    public static Vector2 operator -(Vector2 a, Vector2 b) => new(a.X - b.X, a.Y - b.Y);
    public static Vector2 operator *(Vector2 v, float k) => new(v.X * k, v.Y * k);
    public static Vector2 operator /(Vector2 v, float k) => new(v.X / k, v.Y / k);

    public float Length => MathF.Sqrt(X * X + Y * Y);
    public Vector2 Normalized => Length > 0 ? this / Length : this;
    public static float Dot(Vector2 a, Vector2 b) => a.X * b.X + a.Y * b.Y;

    public override string ToString() => $"({X}, {Y})";
}
```

It is a `readonly struct`, so a vector never changes in place: `a + b` builds a new one. Unlike Unity's `Vector2`, that rules out the property-copy trap from the C# lesson entirely.

